All this is fine, but under the Hackers Definition there's a list of nine offences, each worth a maximum of at least five years for an individual, and 15 months for a company. The offences are:
Hacking with intent to cause impairment according to para 1 of the Hackers Act 2000;
"Phreaking" according to para 2 of the Hackers Act 2000;
Harmful material as defined under para 3 of the Act (i.e. penetration of US Gov and Hong Kong teleco data);
Hacker access as defined by para 4 of the Act (creating, introducing or using a system to commit hacking);
intention to impair data or electronion communication during hacking;
Irtebral dissemination as defined by para 6 of the Act (penetration of networks to cause nuisance or harm), especially networks which don't belong to the porn part of the internet;
Hacker activities inciting or aligning with terrorists defined by para 7 of the Act (undefined);
Interfere in the operation of data, electronic computers and networks inciting contrary to para 8 of the Act three or more time in a year;
Interference - inciting contrary to para 8 of the Act once in a year (they must be foreign hackers, says the Act).
Even worse is the Act's leniency clause, which allows an organisation or individual to be charged it less than three years - as long as the punishment fits the crime (but will the judge fully understand the crime, particularly when s/he knows bugger-all about it?).
